

There are possible issues with strtold as seen in the manpage :

RETURN VALUE
    These functions return the converted value, if any.

    If endptr is not NULL, a pointer to the character after the last
character used in the conversion is stored in the location referenced
by endptr.

    If no conversion is performed, zero is returned and (unless endptr
is null) the value of nptr is stored in the location referenced by
endptr.

   If the correct value would cause overflow, plus or minus HUGE_VAL,
HUGE_VALF, or HUGE_VALL is returned (according to the return type and
sign of the value), and ERANGE is stored in errno.

   If the correct value would cause overflow, plus or minus HUGE_VAL,
HUGE_VALF, or HUGE_VALL is returned (according to the sign and type of
the return value), and ERANGE is stored in errno. If the correct value
would cause underflow, zero is returned and ERANGE is stored in errno.

   The value of the environment variable LC_NUMERIC will determine the
character to be interpreted as a decimal point.

ERRORS : ERANGE Overflow or underflow may occur.

NOTES
    The value 0 can legitimately be returned on both success and failure
therefore the calling program should set errno to 0 before the call. You
may then determine if an error occurred by checking whether errno has a
nonzero value after the call.
